    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP, today\u2019s slate is headlined by **Corbin Carroll** (77.56) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91), who represent our highest-ceiling plays against favorable arms. **Corbin Carroll** remains our premier offensive engine, though his efficiency is tempered slightly by a high-holding opposing catcher; nevertheless, his role in the #3 spot against Clay Holmes keeps his score elite. **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits significantly from a favorable park factor and a strong order position, providing a massive boost to our daily output potential. **Trevor Story** (71.91) also presents a high-value start, as favorable winds and a solid matchup against Nick Martinez allow him to punch above his typical base projection.\n\nOur platoon management is focused on capitalizing on specific SP_xERA vulnerabilities. **Josh Naylor** (68.04) draws a favorable matchup against the struggling Anthony Kay, and his high SP skill adjustment compensates for a minor platoon disadvantage. Conversely, **Mickey Moniak** (66.49) is being started despite a tough matchup against Aaron Nola; his score is buoyed by a prime batting order slot and a favorable dynamic platoon split. While **Jose Caballero** (56.90) and **Brice Turang** (53.16) hover in the marginal tier, their involvement is tactical\u2014we are leaning into their profiles to maintain our edge in the scarce stolen base category, even when the underlying pitching matchup is neutral.\n\nThe most difficult decision was the exclusion of **Cole Young** (52.21), who sat just outside the active roster due to a poor projected batting order position. While his matchup against Anthony Kay is attractive, his projected #9 spot effectively capped his efficiency below the threshold of our lower-tier starters like **Alex Freeland** (52.76). Additionally, we are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for several key pieces; specifically, the confirmed placements of **Gabriel Moreno** and **Dalton Rushing** will be critical, as any downward shift in their order could threaten their current marginal status. We are playing it safe by keeping **Austin Riley** on the bench, as his matchup against Blake Snell provides insufficient upside to justify risking our daily game caps.",
